// Binding a function of the WRONG SHAPE, in a position that was never signature-checked. `emitFnPtrBind`
// runs `sigMatches` and is called from a local declaration only, so this argument bind reached
// `emitExpression`'s bare function-name arm instead and was accepted in silence — and the call through
// the pointer then passed one argument to a two-parameter function. That is an indirect call through a
// mismatched function-pointer type: undefined behavior, and precisely the class UBSan's `function` check
// would report, which is disabled suite-wide for an unrelated reason. Neither the C compiler nor the
// sanitizers were going to catch it here.
fnptr int32 F(int32 a);
fn int32 two(int32 a, int32 b) { return a + b; }
fn int32 run(F f, int32 n) { return f(a: n); }
fn int32 main() { return run(f: two, n: 1); }
